Found during the B019 parent review, 2026-08-30. The bug class it guards is
newly load-bearing as of B019.

## Why this matters now
Before B019, `DEPENDENCY_<ID>` was defined for every dependency DECLARED in
info.toml, so a `#if DEPENDENCY_X` branch was live regardless of what was
installed. After B019 the define is gated on the dependency actually
RESOLVING — which means **test outcomes now depend on which plugins exist in
`~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins` on the machine running them.**

One instance already slipped in and was caught only by a review agent:
`check_command_applies_manifest_defines_and_dependency_defines` asserted a
`#if DEPENDENCY_EDITOR` branch was live. `Editor` happens to be installed on
the dev box, so it passed here and would have failed for anyone without it —
including CI. It is now hermetic (the dependency is provisioned inside the
test's own fixture dir, with an explicit `--plugins-dir` and an isolated
`HOME`).

The suite is currently clean: verified by running all 16 test binaries with
`HOME`/`USERPROFILE` pointed at an empty directory — 1426 tests, 0 failures —
with the method itself validated by confirming it FAILS on the pre-fix tree.
See the B019 ticket for the full procedure.

**But nothing preserves that property.** The check was manual and one-off. The
next test that reads a real plugin install will pass on the author's machine
and fail for everyone else, and the failure will look like flakiness rather
than a hermeticity bug.

## Work
- Add a gate that runs the suite under an isolated `HOME`, so a test depending
  on a local Openplanet install or user config fails at the point it is
  written rather than on someone else's machine.
- Note the constraint that shaped the manual procedure: `cargo test` cannot
  simply be run with a fake `HOME`, because rustup needs the real one to
  locate a toolchain. The working approach is `cargo test --no-run` to build
  and enumerate the test binaries, then execute each directly with `HOME` and
  `USERPROFILE` pointed at an empty dir. Reuse that shape.
- Wire it into the repo's own gate — a `justfile` recipe (e.g. `test-hermetic`)
  and CI, whichever CI this repo uses. Do not invent a parallel test runner.
- Both `~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins` (auto-detected plugins dir) and
  `~/.config/openplanet-lsp/config.toml` (user config) are HOME-derived, so
  isolating HOME covers both surfaces.

## Design decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)
The hermetic run is an ADDITIONAL gate, not a replacement for the normal one.
Some tests legitimately exercise auto-detection against a real install; if any
such test exists or is added later, it must be explicitly marked (a feature
gate or an `#[ignore]` with a named reason) rather than silently relied upon.
A test that needs a real install is fine; a test that needs one by accident is
the bug.

Do NOT make the hermetic run the default `just test` — the normal run is
faster and is what developers iterate against.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: write a deliberately non-hermetic test (asserts on a plugin that exists
only in a real `~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins`), confirm it PASSES under the normal
gate and FAILS under the new hermetic gate. Then delete it, or keep it behind
an ignore as the gate's own self-test — decide and document which.

This red step is the whole point: a hermeticity gate that cannot fail is worse
than none, because it manufactures confidence. The B019 lane validated its
manual procedure exactly this way (archived the pre-fix tree, confirmed the
offending test failed under a clean HOME) and that is the standard here.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- a test reading `~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins` -> caught
- a test reading `~/.config/openplanet-lsp/config.toml` -> caught
- a test reading `$USERPROFILE` (the Windows-style path branch) -> caught
- a test using an absolute path OUTSIDE HOME -> NOT caught by HOME isolation;
  document this limit explicitly rather than implying full coverage (every
  `--plugins-dir` in the suite currently points inside a temp fixture, which
  is what makes the gate sufficient today)
- a test depending on the ABSENCE of an installed plugin -> would fail on a
  dev box and pass in CI; note it as the inverse case the gate cannot catch
- the gate itself passes on a clean checkout with no Openplanet installed at
  all (the CI case)

## Related
B019 (merged, 2f38818) — made the define set depend on actual resolution, and
carries the full hermeticity procedure and its stated limits. B040 will add
manifest diagnostics, another surface where a fixture could reach outside
itself.